---
layout: base.njk
title: Start your studio
description: "Start your Prospektor workspace: $999 a month or $9,990 a year, unlimited runs, your whole team in. Next stop is Stripe, and the research runs while you pay."
# One page per language (#114) — see src/index.njk for the reasoning.
pagination:
  data: languages
  size: 1
  alias: language
  addAllPagesToCollections: true
permalink: "{{ language.prefix }}/checkout/"
---

<section class="onboard">
  <div class="onboard-inner">

    <ol class="steps" id="steps">
      <li class="step done"><span class="step-n-badge">✓</span>{% t %}Scan{% endt %}</li>
      <li class="step active" data-step="target"><span class="step-n-badge">2</span>{% t %}Your target{% endt %}</li>
      <li class="step" data-step="pay"><span class="step-n-badge">3</span>{% t %}Payment{% endt %}</li>
      {#- No data-step: step 4 happens on /checkout/done/, not on this page,
          so the step driver must never mark it active or done here. -#}
      <li class="step"><span class="step-n-badge">4</span>{% t %}Sign in{% endt %}</li>
    </ol>

    <!-- STEP 2: confirm the target -->
    <div class="onboard-panel" id="panelTarget">
      <div class="scan-result-meta" id="obMeta"></div>
      {#- One <h1> per document (#137). These are three steps of one flow, only one
        of them visible at a time, but a crawler sees the whole page at once and
        read three competing <h1>s. The class carries the styling, so the steps
        look identical. -#}
      {#- #242: short enough to sit on one line at every width — the old
          "Here's what Prospektor will hunt for you." wrapped mid-phrase. -#}
      <h1 class="onboard-h1">{% t %}Your target.{% endt %}</h1>
      <p class="onboard-sub">{% t %}Sharpen it in your own words — it seeds your studio's brief.{% endt %}</p>
      <!-- Direct path (no scan): the studio researches the buyer from their
           site, so the one thing we must have is their address. -->
      <div id="siteAsk" hidden>
        <p class="onboard-sub">{% t %}Came here directly? All we need is your website — your studio researches you from it. (Or <a href="/#scan">scan it first</a> to see what it finds, free.){% endt %}</p>
        <input class="reserve-input" id="siteInput" type="text" inputmode="url" autocomplete="url"
               placeholder="yourcompany.com" aria-label="{% t %}Your website{% endt %}">
        <p class="reserve-msg is-error" id="siteMsg" hidden></p>
      </div>
      <textarea class="goal-input" id="goalInput" rows="4" placeholder="{% t %}What should Prospektor find for you? e.g. Mid-size property managers in the DACH region who still screen tenants manually.{% endt %}"></textarea>
      <div class="onboard-actions">
        <button class="btn-cta" id="toPayBtn">{% t %}Looks right — continue →{% endt %}</button>
        <a class="onboard-back" href="{{ '/#scan' | localize }}">{% t %}← rescan a different site{% endt %}</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- STEP 3: payment -->
    <div class="onboard-panel" id="panelPay" hidden>
      <h2 class="onboard-h1">{% t %}Payment.{% endt %}</h2>
      <p class="onboard-sub">{% t %}The moment payment lands, your studio starts researching you.{% endt %}</p>
      <div class="order-card">
        {#- #542 — the same switch /pricing/ carries, and the same rule: both
            figures are written here by the build and plan.js only hides one of
            them, so the order card can never show a price the checkout call
            does not send. `?plan=year` from /pricing/ arrives preselected. -#}
        <div class="plan-switch" id="planSwitch" role="group" aria-label="{% t %}Billing period{% endt %}">
          <button type="button" class="plan-opt" data-plan="month" aria-pressed="true">{% t %}Monthly{% endt %}</button>
          <button type="button" class="plan-opt" data-plan="year" aria-pressed="false">{% t %}Yearly — 2 months free{% endt %}</button>
        </div>
        <div class="order-row">
          <span class="order-name">{% t %}Prospektor · one workspace{% endt %}</span>
          <span class="order-price" data-plan-show="month">$999<span class="order-per">/mo</span></span>
          <span class="order-price" data-plan-show="year" hidden>$9,990<span class="order-per">/yr</span></span>
        </div>
        <p class="plan-note" data-plan-show="year" hidden>{% t %}<strong>Two months free</strong> on $11,988 — charged once, and the price is fixed for the year.{% endt %}</p>
        <ul class="pricing-list">
          <li>{% t %}Unlimited pitch runs, under fair use{% endt %}</li>
          <li>{% t %}Deck, one-pager, cold emails and proposal — every run, in your voice{% endt %}</li>
          <li>{% t %}Your whole team in via your work-email domain{% endt %}</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <!-- Live checkout, revealed by checkout.js only when the server says
           Stripe keys exist. Until then the founding-spot capture below is
           the payment step, exactly as before. -->
      <div id="stripePay" hidden>
        <!-- #243: the field says what it is FOR before asking for anything —
             the operator read the bare input as "why do you want my email?".
             The Google door exists, but it is the studio's sign-in after
             payment, not an account here: one line says so instead of a
             second auth path this page does not need (less-is-more). -->
        <label class="pay-label" for="payEmail">{% t %}Your sign-in email{% endt %}</label>
        <form class="reserve-form" id="stripeForm" novalidate>
          <input class="reserve-input" id="payEmail" type="email" autocomplete="email"
                 placeholder="you@yourcompany.com" aria-label="{% t %}Your sign-in email{% endt %}">
          <button class="btn-cta" type="submit" id="stripeBtn">{% t %}Continue to secure checkout →{% endt %}</button>
          <!-- #204: the same unticked, optional box the studio's first-run card
               carries, asked here because the buyer is already filling in a
               form. It stays unticked and nothing depends on it — a tick rides
               through checkout metadata and the studio records the permission
               against this address, so the buyer is not asked again inside.
               #243 asked for it pre-ticked: NOT done — a pre-ticked consent
               box is invalid for EU visitors (GDPR/ePrivacy, Planet49); the
               question is with COMPLIANCE in HANDOVER-compliance.md. -->
          <label class="pay-opt" for="payMarketing">
            <input type="checkbox" id="payMarketing">
            <span>{% t %}Product news and tips, by email — optional, a few times a month at most{% endt %}</span>
          </label>
        </form>
        <p class="onboard-sub">{% t %}After payment you sign in with this address — with Google, or an emailed link. Work email means your whole team gets in.{% endt %}</p>
        <p class="reserve-msg is-error" id="stripeMsg" hidden></p>
      </div>
      <div class="onboard-note" id="payFallbackNote">
        <p>{% t %}<strong>Checkout isn't open yet — days, not weeks.</strong> Leave your email and we'll hold a founding spot; you'll get one message when checkout opens, nothing else. Your target sentence rides along so your studio starts from it.{% endt %}</p>
      </div>
      <form class="reserve-form" id="reserveForm" novalidate>
        <input class="reserve-input" id="reserveEmail" type="email" autocomplete="email"
               placeholder="you@yourcompany.com" aria-label="{% t %}Your work email{% endt %}">
        <input type="text" name="hp" id="reserveHp" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" style="position:absolute;left:-9999px;" aria-hidden="true">
        <button class="btn-cta" type="submit" id="reserveBtn">{% t %}Hold my founding spot{% endt %}</button>
      </form>
      <!-- Static twin of the capture form so Netlify registers it at deploy
           time; checkout.js posts to it when the email function fails. -->
      <form name="founding-spot" netlify netlify-honeypot="hp" hidden>
        <input type="email" name="email">
        <input type="text" name="domain">
        <input type="text" name="company">
        <textarea name="goal"></textarea>
        <input type="text" name="hp">
      </form>
      <p class="reserve-msg" id="reserveMsg" hidden></p>
    </div>
    {#- #243: the "what happens after payment →" preview button and its step-4
        panel are gone — one primary action per screen (less-is-more), and
        /checkout/done/ now tells the sign-in story to the person it is
        actually for, after they have actually paid. The step bar keeps
        "Sign in" so payment is never the apparent last step. -#}

  </div>
</section>
{#- defer (#137). This sat mid-document without it, so the parser stopped
    here and rendered nothing below until the script had been fetched and
    run. Every one of these is an IIFE that looks its elements up by id and
    returns if they are absent, so deferring is not only cheaper — it is the
    case the guard was written for, with the DOM guaranteed complete. -#}
<script src="{{ '/assets/js/checkout.js' | asset }}" defer></script>
{#- After checkout.js, for the reason given on /pricing/ (#542). -#}
<script src="{{ '/assets/js/plan.js' | asset }}" defer></script>
